    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assistant-teaching-professor-of-animation/33874br": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Animation",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assistant Teaching Professor of Animation Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assistant Teaching Professor of Animation Position Overview The Department of Design at the University of Kansas is seeking a faculty member at the Assistant Teaching Professor level to work with undergraduate students in its newly established program in Animation. We are looking for an enthusiastic and engaging design thinker and maker with a passion for visual storytelling, character animation, and the development and exploration of animation tools and techniques, who is interested in working closely with our faculty to build this new program. Candidates with experience in teaching, curriculum development, and robust professional practice will stand out. We also encourage recent master’s degree graduates eager to launch a career in animation education, or education and mentorship minded industry professional to apply. Candidates must be able to demonstrate professional level skills in 2D or 3D animation practice with a focus on narrative and character animation, complemented by a strong historical familiarity with the medium, and a passion for educating students in these areas. Candidates should also have a strong background in industry standard production tools and methods, and current professional practice standards. Ideal candidates will demonstrate a proficiency in one or more of the following areas: 2D and/or 3D character animation – body mechanics, acting, dialogue, character animation workflow and tools. Directing for animation – storyboarding, visual storytelling. 3D Skills – modeling, rigging, texturing, or lighting for animation and games. Animation production and project management. This position is subject to a collective bargaining agreement between The University of Kansas and United Academics, United Academics of KU, AAUP, or AFT. UAKU is the exclusive representative. As the State of Kansas is a right to work state, you have the right to be a member or not be a member of a union without fear of personal or professional repercussions. About the Department of Design The Department of Design, housed in Chalmers Hall, prepares animators, illustrators, experience designers, graphic designers, marketing creatives, motion designers, product designers, UX/UI developers, and other multidisciplinary communicators to thrive as innovative practitioners, and socially engaged leaders, in an ever-changing professional field. Design students have access to studio spaces, computer labs, a letterpress lab, a Riso lab, and digital fabrication labs. Students gain insights from lectures and workshops with world-renowned designers, collaborations with industry partners, and opportunities to participate in short-term study abroad programs and semester-long internships in the US and abroad. Students earn a BFA in Design with programs in  Animation, Illustration, Industrial Design, Visual Communication Design, and Interaction Design. The Bachelor of Fine Arts in Design is a four-year, 120-credit-hour degree program. All design students spend the first semester in general design foundation courses and in the second semester begin taking foundation courses in their chosen major. KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe. At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate, and support each other’s success. We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued. When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things.  This is a non-tenure position with up to a 3-year limited term contract, which has the possibility of renewal based upon a comprehensive evaluation. #LI-HR1 Job Description The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. by the effective date of the appointment. Allocation of effort: 1.0 full-time equivalent appointment will be distributed as follows: Teaching/Advising (70%) – Teach required courses in animation program, as well as animation courses open to all other majors, at the undergraduate level. The typical teaching expectation for this position is a 3/3 course load. Participate in the planning and integration of existing and new course work & technologies in the Animation undergraduate program. Candidates must also meet department expectations in terms of student advising. Service (20%) – Serve the department, university, state, and the profession. This includes attending departmental meetings, participating in committees and boards, and performing select administrative departmental functions. Research and/or Applied Professional Practice (10%) – Conduct research, creative, and/or professional work that focusses on animation and its application. Collaborate with clients on the application of this work and/or disseminate this work through peer-reviewed publications and conferences. Required Qualifications Master's degree in animation, illustration, entertainment design, film, visual communications, or a related field. Candidates with a bachelor's degree in animation or a related field with 5 years demonstrated professional experience will also be considered. Industry professionals with an interest in academia are encouraged to apply. A strong portfolio demonstrating expertise in teaching, research, and/or industry experience, specifically in the areas of 2D/3D animation and related fields. Ability to demonstrate a commitment to collaborate and support faculty colleagues, develop curriculum, and help expand animation studio offerings. Demonstrated excellent written and oral communication skills and a strong collaborative spirit for teamwork and service. This position requires a formal degree in the cited discipline area(s) to ensure that candidates have the foundational knowledge and skill set required to perform the duties of the position. The level of degree is based on accreditation requirements, institutional standards of academic and professional excellence, alignment with peer institution benchmarks, and contributes to the credibility and effectiveness of the role within the university community. Additional Candidate Instructions A complete application will include the following items: Cover letter addressing qualifications Resume/CV Statement of teaching philosophy References: the names, addresses and phone numbers of at least three references qualified to evaluate your professional experiences and achievements. A representative visual on-line portfolio of creative work and/or personal work. A representative on-line portfolio of student work. Supporting materials such as links to websites, videos, research, blogs, prints, books, etc. can be submitted as links in addition to on-line portfolios. Application review began Monday, November 2, 2026 and will continue until position is filled Contact Information to Applicants Jeremy Shellhorn, Chair of the Department of Design jeshellh@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range $64,000 - 72,000. Group insurance plan, including medical, dental, and hospital coverage is available. Application Review Begins Monday November 2, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Wednesday August 18, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33874BR Department: &nbspDesign Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspOn-Site Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp02-Nov-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
    },
    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assistant-teaching-professor-of-visual-communication-design/33926br": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Visual Communication Design",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assistant Teaching Professor of Visual Communication Design Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assistant Teaching Professor of Visual Communication Design Position Overview The Department of Design at the University of Kansas is seeking a faculty member at the Assistant Teaching Professor level to teach undergraduate and graduate students in its Visual Communication Design and MDES programs and help develop new curricular offerings in Experiential Graphic Design (XGD). We are looking for an enthusiastic and engaging design thinker, maker, and educator with a passion for connecting people to place through visual communication in the built environment. The successful candidate will work closely with faculty to strengthen the program's integration of typography, information design, identity systems, interaction, and spatial experience. Candidates with experience in teaching, curriculum development, collaborative practice, and robust professional work will stand out. We also encourage recent terminal-degree graduates eager to launch a career in design education and education- and mentorship-minded industry professionals to apply. Candidates must be able to demonstrate professional-level skills in visual communication design and experiential graphic design, supported by a strong foundation in typography, visual systems, information design, branding, human-centered research, accessibility, and production. They should be able to guide students from research and concept development through prototyping, fabrication, implementation, and evaluation. Candidates should also have a strong understanding of industry-standard tools, materials, production methods, and current professional practice, along with the ability to collaborate across graphic design, architecture, interior design, industrial design, interaction design, and related disciplines. Ideal candidates will demonstrate proficiency in one or more of the following areas: wayfinding systems, including signs, maps, directories, and directional cues that help people move safely and intuitively through complex public environments; branded environments that translate organizational identity and culture into spatial graphics and architectural settings; and exhibitions and placemaking, including interpretive panels, interactive displays, public art, and installations that transform spaces into engaging physical stories. Additional strengths may include environmental graphics, mapping and information systems, inclusive and universal design, digital and interactive experiences, 3D visualization, fabrication, project management, and collaboration with clients, communities, architects, fabricators, and allied professionals. This position is subject to a collective bargaining agreement between The University of Kansas and United Academics, United Academics of KU, AAUP, or AFT. UAKU is the exclusive representative. As the State of Kansas is a right to work state, you have the right to be a member or not be a member of a union without fear of personal or professional repercussions. KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe. At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate, and support each other’s success. We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued. When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things.  This is a non-tenure position with up to a 3-year limited term contract, which has the possibility of renewal based upon a comprehensive evaluation. #LI-HR1 Job Description About the Department of Design The Department of Design, housed in Chalmers Hall, prepares animators, illustrators, experience designers, graphic designers, marketing creatives, motion designers, photographers, product designers, UX/UI developers, and other multidisciplinary communicators to thrive as innovative practitioners and socially engaged leaders in an ever-changing professional field. Design students have access to studio spaces, computer labs, a letterpress lab, a Riso lab, a robust photography area, and digital fabrication labs. Students gain insights from lectures and workshops with world-renowned designers, collaborations with industry partners, and opportunities to participate in short-term study abroad programs and semester-long internships in the US and abroad. Students earn a BFA in Design with programs in Animation, Illustration, Industrial Design, Visual Communication Design, and Interaction Design. The Bachelor of Fine Arts in Design is a four-year, 120-credit-hour degree program. All design students spend the first semester in general design foundation courses and in the second semester begin taking foundation courses in their chosen major. KU Design also offers minors in Design and Design Entrepreneurship, as well as a Certificate in Book Arts. Our graduate programs offer an M.A. in Interaction and User Experience Design and an M.A. in Design Management and Strategy. The Master of Arts in Design is 31 credit hours of coursework, a thesis that documents independent discovery and research, and an oral examination. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. by the effective date of the appointment. Allocation of effort: 1.0 full-time equivalent appointment will be distributed as follows: Teaching/Advising (70%) - Teach required courses in the Visual Communication Design program, including courses focused on experiential graphic design, as well as visual communication courses open to students from other majors at the undergraduate level. The typical teaching expectation for this position is a 3/3 course load. Courses may include typographic systems, identity systems, information design, wayfinding, branded environments, exhibition design, placemaking, and related studios. Participate in the planning and integration of existing and new coursework, technologies, fabrication methods, and community or industry partnerships. Candidates must also meet department expectations for student advising. Service (20%) - Serve the department, university, state, and profession. This includes attending departmental meetings, participating in committees and boards, supporting recruitment and outreach, and performing select administrative departmental functions. Research and/or Applied Professional Practice (10%) - Conduct research, creative, and/or professional work focused on experiential graphic design and its applications. Collaborate with clients, communities, and professional partners on the application of this work and/or disseminate the work through exhibitions, publications, presentations, conferences, and other appropriate venues. Required Qualifications MFA or terminal degree in graphic design, visual communication design, experiential or environmental graphic design, communication design, interaction design, architecture, interior design, or a related field. Candidates with a bachelor's degree in visual communication design or a related field and 9 or more years of professional experience will also be considered.  Any successful candidate without a Master’s degree will require approval from the Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs. Industry professionals with an interest in academia are encouraged to apply. A strong portfolio demonstrating expertise in teaching, research, creative practice, and/or industry experience, particularly in wayfinding, branded environments, exhibitions, placemaking, or related areas of experiential graphic design. Demonstrated command of typography, visual systems, information design, accessibility, and human-centered design, along with proficiency in relevant industry-standard tools, prototyping methods, fabrication processes, and production practices. Ability to collaborate with and support faculty colleagues, develop curriculum, mentor students in multidisciplinary projects, and help expand experiential graphic design studio offerings within the Visual Communication Design program. Demonstrated excellent written and oral communication skills and a strong collaborative spirit for teamwork, teaching, mentorship, and service. This position requires a formal degree in the cited discipline area(s) to ensure that candidates have the foundational knowledge and skill set required to perform the duties of the position. The level of degree is based on accreditation requirements, institutional standards of academic and professional excellence, alignment with peer institution benchmarks, and contributes to the credibility and effectiveness of the role within the university community. Additional Candidate Instructions A complete application will include the following items: Cover letter addressing qualifications and interest in the position Resume/CV Statement of teaching philosophy and approach to experiential graphic design education References: the names, addresses, email addresses, and phone numbers of at least three references qualified to evaluate your professional experiences and achievements A representative online portfolio of professional, creative, research, and/or personal work A representative online portfolio of student work or other evidence of teaching and mentorship Supporting materials such as links to websites, videos, research, exhibitions, publications, fabrication documentation, or other relevant work may be submitted in addition to the online portfolios. Application review begins Monday, November 16, 2026 and will continue until position is filled. Contact Information to Applicants Jeremy Shellhorn, Chair of the Department of Design jeshellh@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range $64,000 - 72,000. Group insurance plan, including medical, dental, and hospital coverage is available. Application Review Begins Monday November 16, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Wednesday August 18, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33926BR Department: &nbspDesign Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspOn-Site Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp16-Nov-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
    },
    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assistant-associate-professor-of-architecture/33493br": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Architecture",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assistant/Associate Professor of Architecture Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assistant/Associate Professor of Architecture Position Overview The School of Architecture & Design at the University of Kansas invites applications for a tenure track or tenured position as Assistant or Associate Professor of Architecture, with an emphasis in Computational/Parametric Design and Advanced Manufacturing Technologies . The appointment is expected to begin in the spring semester of 2027. Architects are increasingly expected to integrate advanced computational design technologies, digital workflows, and research into the design, coordination, fabrication, and performance of buildings. This expertise spans the integration of building systems and programs through computational and parametric design, artificial intelligence, building performance analysis, advanced digital fabrication, and industrialized construction processes. This position will play a central role in developing and implementing coursework across the professional and graduate curricula in these areas, fostering externally funded research in computational design, digital fabrication, and emerging design technologies, and coordinating professional and academic programs that support certifications and advanced credentials in automated design processes and digital practice. The successful candidate should demonstrate expertise in one or more of the following areas: Computational and parametric design, advanced digital fabrication, industrialized construction, building performance technologies, artificial intelligence integration in architectural design, or related emerging digital technologies that advance architectural practice and research. The faculty is committed to excellence in applying its knowledge and experience to educating the next generation of architects. The Department seeks candidates who will contribute to this mission through innovative teaching, impactful scholarship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and engagement with the evolving profession. KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe.  At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate and support each other’s success.  We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued.  When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things.  The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. by the effective date of the appointment. This position is subject to a collective bargaining agreement between The University of Kansas and United Academics, United Academics of KU, AAUP, or AFT. UAKU is the exclusive representative. As the State of Kansas is a right to work state, you have the right to be a member or not be a member of a union without fear of personal or professional repercussions. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the United States by the effective date of the appointment. About the University Founded in 1865, the University of Kansas is a designated Carnegie comprehensive doctoral research university and one of the public members of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU). The University enrolls more than 30,000 students and offers students and faculty opportunities to collaborate in its graduate programs which include education, public health, medicine, law, the humanities, and a number of allied social science disciplines. Situated in the historic, diverse, and culturally vibrant city of Lawrence, Kansas, 40 minutes from the Kansas City metropolitan area, the University of Kansas’ Department of Architecture has a 109-year history of educating architects who have made significant contributions to the state, region, and world. Job Description Allocation of Effort: Teaching, 40 percent The typical teaching expectation for Architecture faculty is two 6-credit upper-level architectural design studios and one 3-credit lecture or seminar per year. The successful candidate will also meet departmental expectations for student advising. Research, 40 percent Conduct research, creative work, and/or professional work focusses on advanced manufacturing technologies, and related areas of digital practice. Disseminate this work through peer-reviewed publications, exhibitions, conferences, and/or other appropriate scholarly and professional venues. Seek external funding to support a focused research agenda. Service, 20 percent Serve the department, university, state, and profession, primarily through committees, boards, and related forms of professional and institutional service. Required Qualifications Required Qualifications for Both Assistant and Associate Professor Candidates Demonstrated record of research, creative work, and/or professional work that demonstrates expertise in computational/parametric design, advanced manufacturing processes and related areas of digital practice. Significant professional experience in architectural practice plus a master’s degree in architecture, or a PhD in architecture with an emphasis in computational/parametric design and advanced manufacturing processes. A professional degree in architecture, NAAB-accredited or equivalent. Required Qualifications for Associate Professor Candidates Only Established record of teaching consistent with the department’s standards for promotion to the rank of Associate Professor of Architecture. Established record of research, creative work, and/or professional work consistent with the department’s standards for promotion to the rank of Associate Professor of Architecture. Additional Candidate Instructions Applicants should submit the following items: Letter of intent which includes a statement outlining their area of expertise, research, creative, and/or professional interests and shows how these will contribute to the degree programs in the Architecture Department at the University of Kansas; Curriculum vitae; Names and contact information (email included) of three references; and A portfolio that includes examples of professional, academic (teaching and research), and any other relevant work.  Please upload candidate package and indicate in the letter of intent a URL to access the complete digital version of the portfolio. Application review will begin September 30th and will remain open until the position is filled. Contact Information to Applicants Marie-Alice L’Heureux malheur@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range commensurate with experience Application Review Begins Wednesday September 30, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Friday January 1, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33493BR Department: &nbspArchitecture Design Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspOn-Site Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp30-Sep-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
    },
    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assistant-associate-professor-of-the-practice-architecture/33955br": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of the Practice - Architecture",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assistant/Associate Professor of the Practice - Architecture Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assistant/Associate Professor of the Practice - Architecture Position Overview The School of Architecture and Design at the University of Kansas invites applications for a part- or full-time (80%-100%), non-tenure-track position of Assistant/Associate Professor of Practice with expertise in integrated design, technology in design, AI in professional practice, sustainability, project management, and/or other significant areas of professional practice. The appointment is expected to begin in the spring semester of 2027. This position may be appointed for up to an initial three-year period with an opportunity for extension/renewal after a comprehensive review. The teaching responsibilities of this position will be focused on studio instruction in the accredited Master of Architecture curriculum. The successful candidate for this position is expected to have significant professional experience as outlined in the Required Qualifications. The Architecture Department faculty’s core purpose is to prepare new practitioners in the design of the built environment, and for the path to architectural licensure. It seeks ways by which humanity can inhabit this planet in a sustainably healthy way. It does so primarily through its NAAB (National Architectural Accrediting Board) accredited Master of Architecture (M.Arch) program and its accredited Interior Architecture program. The faculty is committed to excellence in applying our knowledge and experience in educating the next generation of architects. The Department seeks candidates who can contribute to its knowledge and teaching as described above. In addition to the M.Arch and IA degrees, the Architecture Department also offers an a PhD in architecture and multiple certificate programs. It has summer, semester, and year-long study abroad programs around the globe. The Architecture Department has recognized areas of expertise across Design-build, Sports and Leisure, Health and Wellness, Interior Architecture, Urban Housing, Urban Design, Aviation and Transit, Historic Preservation, and Sustainable Design, among others. About the University of Kansas The University of Kansas is the state’s flagship university, an R-1 institution and one of just 71 invited members of the prestigious Association of American Universities. Our university consistently earns high rankings for academics and recognition as a premier research university (engaging in $611 million of research in fiscal year 2025). KU has 25 programs ranked in the U.S. News & World Report top 25 among public universities, including #1 rankings for graduate programs in paleontology and special education. KU has 65 ranked graduate programs and is ranked 31st in best value among public institutions in 2026. KU offers more than 8,000 courses, 460 degree and certificate programs, and more than 200 fields of study. The University values global engagement with more than 150 programs of international study and cooperative research and offers more than 200 study abroad programs. As of Fall 2025, student enrollment for the Lawrence-Edwards campus was 27,212 representing all 50 states and 115 countries. This position is subject to a collective bargaining agreement between The University of Kansas and United Academics, United Academics of KU, AAUP, or AFT. UAKU is the exclusive representative. As the State of Kansas is a right to work state, you have the right to be a member or not be a member of a union without fear of personal or professional repercussions. Job Description KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe. At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate, and support each other’s success. We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued. When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the United States by the effective date of the appointment. Allocation of Effort: Teaching (80%) The typical teaching expectation for Professors of Practice in Architecture is 12 credit hours per semester. This equates to two 6-credit hour studios OR one 6-credit hour studio and two 3-credit hour courses per semester. Specific course assignments and locations are at the discretion of the Chair of the Architecture Department, although candidates may be consulted about their preferences. Teaching typically, but not exclusively, involves being an instructor of record for courses, but may include team teaching, carrying out course transformation, assessment of learning outcomes, or other activities to implement innovations in teaching and learning. Service (20%) Serve the department, university, state, and/or the profession. This includes attending departmental meetings and serving on committees (as appropriate). All faculty members are expected to follow the school’s standards of professionalism. Required Qualifications Required Qualifications for Both Assistant and Associate Professor Candidates Demonstrated record of professional work and/or creative work that demonstrates expertise in a range of building typologies, consultation, and/or project management in practice. At least 9 years of professional experience. Architectural licensure. A professional degree in architecture (Bachelor’s-level or higher), NAAB-accredited or equivalent. Any successful candidate without a Master’s degree will require approval from the Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs. Required Qualifications for Associate Professor Candidates Only Sustained record of teaching and/or professional mentoring and team leadership demonstrating consistent performance across several semesters. Sustained record of professional work and/or creative work with at least 10 years of professional experience. This position requires a formal degree in the cited discipline area(s) to ensure that candidates have the foundational knowledge and skill set required to perform the duties of the position. The level of degree is based on accreditation requirements, institutional standards of academic and professional excellence, alignment with peer institution benchmarks, and contributes to the credibility and effectiveness of the role within the university community. Additional Candidate Instructions Applicants should submit the following items: 1)  Letter of intent, including a statement outlining their area of expertise, creative, and/or professional interests, and show how they see these contributing to the degree programs in the Architecture Department at the University of Kansas; 2)  Curriculum Vitae; 3) Names and contact information (email included) of three references; and 4) A digital portfolio of professional, creative, and/or student work to demonstrate effectiveness of teaching. Candidates should provide a link where the portfolio can be viewed and downloaded. Application review begins Tuesday, November 10, 2026. Apply by November 9th to ensure consideration. Contact Information to Applicants Jae Chang, PhD Professor of Architecture Associate Dean for Research, Innovation & Entrepreneurship jdchang@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range Salary for the position will be commensurate with experience and allocation of effort (part- vs full-time). Application Review Begins Tuesday November 10, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Friday January 1, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33955BR Department: &nbspArchitecture Design Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspOn-Site Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp10-Nov-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
    },
    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assistant-associate-full-professor-wastewater-engineering/33925br": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Wastewater Engineering",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Wastewater Engineering Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Wastewater Engineering Position Overview The University of Kansas (KU) Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering (CEAE) seeks an outstanding individual for a tenure-track open rank position in environmental engineering, with a specialty in wastewater engineering. The successful candidate will contribute to the continued development of the department's academic and research programs in environmental engineering and to KU's broader research community. This search is focused on candidates whose scholarship advances wastewater engineering. Relevant areas may include, but are not limited to, advanced physical, chemical, and biological treatment processes; resource and energy recovery; water reuse; emerging contaminants; environmental biotechnology and microbiology; decentralized treatment; process modeling, sensing, automation, and optimization; and resilient and sustainable wastewater systems. The successful candidate will have an earned doctorate in environmental engineering, civil engineering, chemical engineering, or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment. Candidates must demonstrate the potential to develop a collaborative, externally funded research program and to design and teach high-quality undergraduate and graduate courses. The anticipated starting date is August 18, 2027. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. prior to the start of the position. This position is subject to a collective bargaining agreement between the University of Kansas and the United Academics of KU, the exclusive representative for the bargaining unit. Faculty have the right to be a member or not be a member of UAKU without fear of personal or professional repercussions. About the School of Engineering: The University of Kansas School of Engineering (http://engr.ku.edu/) has undergone a significant expansion while taking explicit care to maintain and improve the high-quality student programs the university is known for. The effort involved a multi-faceted approach aimed at growing our facilities, faculty, leadership, and research and, most importantly, supporting students. It also helps industry partners find the new talent, designs, and techniques they need to fuel economic growth and business success. The CEAE Department ( https://ceae.ku.edu/ ) currently has 26 tenured/tenure-track faculty, four teaching professors / professors of practice, one research professor, and approximately 360 undergraduate students and 140 graduate students, with programs leading to bachelors, masters, and doctoral degrees. The department has a strong history of conducting impactful research while focusing on the success of students at all levels. The department hosts state-of-the-art research facilities, including greenhouse and piloting facilities, and has long collaborated with regional municipalities, with conventional and advanced nutrient recovery facilities (Bardenpho processes). Our close proximity to the Kansas City metro area, which is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of architectural, engineering, and construction firms. This location provides rich opportunities to corporate engagement and partnerships. About Lawrence, KS: KU’s research residential campus is in the center of a vibrant and culturally-rich community — a quintessential college town. Visitors, students and new employees from outside the area enjoy discovering the many wonders of Lawrence, including a thriving downtown, a wide variety of local and regional events, area lakes, vibrant arts and music scenes, and an indelible history. Home to over 95,000 people, Lawrence is located 45 minutes west of Kansas City and 30 minutes east of Topeka, the state capital. Along with the music, arts, culture and sports experiences offered at KU and in Lawrence, the short drive to Kansas City provides quick access to historic jazz clubs, museums, world-class music and theater venues, and professional sports teams. Job Description Culture and Community: KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe. At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate and support each other’s success. We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued. When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. by the effective date of the appointment. Job Duties: 40%  Teaching Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in environmental engineering, including courses in wastewater engineering. Develop courses and participate in curriculum development. Supervise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students throughout the course of their studies. 40%  Research Develop and conduct an innovative research program in wastewater engineering. Relevant areas may include, but are not limited to, advanced physical, chemical, and biological treatment processes; resource and energy recovery; water reuse; emerging contaminants; environmental biotechnology and microbiology; decentralized treatment; process modeling, sensing, automation, and optimization; and resilient and sustainable wastewater systems. Establish a strong research agenda leading to external grant support and publication in recognized refereed journals, and participate in local, national, and international professional conferences and other activities appropriate to the discipline. Supervise M.S. and Ph.D. studies. 20%  Service Perform service activities normally expected of university faculty commensurate with rank, including committee service to the Department, School, and University. Also includes national and international service to the profession. Required Qualifications All Ranks: Earned doctorate in environmental engineering, civil engineering, chemical engineering, or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment. Demonstrable expertise in wastewater engineering. Ability to communicate ideas effectively and work well with others, as demonstrated through application materials and interview. Demonstrated potential to develop an externally funded research program and to design and teach high-quality undergraduate and graduate courses. At the rank of Associate Professor: Sustained record of scholarship and research; Evidence of national recognition in the field; Demonstrated experience in leading multidisciplinary teams; A minimum of five years of post-Ph.D. professional experience in a faculty role or an equivalent combination of academic and relevant industry/practice experience. At the rank of Professor: Sustained record of scholarship and research; Evidence of national and international recognition in the field; Demonstrated experience in leading multidisciplinary teams; A minimum of ten years of post-Ph.D. professional experience in a faculty role or an equivalent combination of academic and relevant industry/practice experience, with a sustained record of leadership. Additional Candidate Instructions A complete online application includes the following materials: (1) cover letter; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) research agenda (2-page limit); (4) teaching philosophy statement (2-page limit); and (5) the names and contact information, including email addresses, for three professional references. Application review will begin November 12, 2026, and will continue until a pool of qualified applicants is received. Contact Information to Applicants Search Committee Chair: Justin Hutchison, jhutch@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range Commensurate with experience Application Review Begins Thursday November 12, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Wednesday August 18, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33925BR Department: &nbspCivil/Environ/Arch Engineering Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspOn-Site Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp12-Nov-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
    },
    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assistant-associate-full-professor-in-architectural-engineering/33929br": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Architectural Engineering",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Architectural Engineering Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Architectural Engineering Position Overview The University of Kansas (KU) Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering (CEAE) seeks outstanding candidates for two tenured or tenure-track faculty positions, one at the rank of Assistant Professor and a second position at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor in architectural engineering. The focus areas for these positions are in building electrical power systems and the broader energy areas described below. The successful candidates are expected to contribute to the continued development of academic and research programs in architectural engineering and to the broader research community. For the building electrical power systems position, relevant expertise may include building electrical power distribution and utilization; electrical system design for commercial and institutional buildings; power quality, reliability, and resilience; emergency and standby power systems; distributed energy resources and microgrids; grid modernization; smart building electrical systems; and advanced sensing, monitoring, and controls for building power systems. The successful candidate in this area will strengthen the program's research and teaching capacity in building electrical and power systems and contribute to engagement with industry and the profession. The broader energy areas of interest include integrated building or district energy systems; building-to-grid integration and grid-interactive buildings; distributed energy resource management; building energy resilience; building energy efficiency and decarbonization; environmentally sustainable buildings; renewable energy systems for buildings and communities; grid reliability and energy infrastructure; and net-zero-energy buildings. Successful candidates must have an earned doctorate in architectural, mechanical, electrical, or civil engineering or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment. Candidates must demonstrate the potential to develop collaborative, externally funded research programs and to design and teach high-quality undergraduate and graduate courses. The anticipated starting date is August 18, 2027. Successful candidates must be eligible to work in the U.S. prior to the start of the position. These positions are subject to a collective bargaining agreement between the University of Kansas and the United Academics of KU, the exclusive representative for the bargaining unit. Faculty have the right to be a member or not be a member of UAKU without fear of personal or professional repercussions. The University of Kansas School of Engineering (http://engr.ku.edu/) has undergone a significant expansion while taking explicit care to maintain and improve the high-quality student programs the university is known for. The effort involved a multifaceted approach aimed at growing our facilities, faculty, leadership, and research and, most importantly, supporting students. It also helps industry partners find the new talent, designs, and techniques they need to fuel economic growth and business success. The CEAE Department (https://ceae.ku.edu/) currently has 26 tenured/tenure-track faculty, four teaching professors and professors of practice, one research professor, and approximately 360 undergraduate students and 140 graduate students, with programs leading to bachelor’s, master's, and doctoral degrees. The department has a strong history of conducting impactful research while focusing on the success of students at all levels. KU's ABET-accredited architectural engineering program benefits from the university's close proximity to the Kansas City metro area, which is home to one of the nation’s largest concentrations of architectural, engineering, and construction firms. This location provides rich opportunities for industry engagement, research partnerships, and student learning. Job Description The University of Kansas is the state’s flagship university, an R-1 institution and one of just 71 invited members of the prestigious Association of American Universities. Our university consistently earns high rankings for academics and recognition as a premier research university (engaging in $611 million of research in fiscal year 2025). KU offers more than 8,000 courses, 460 degree and certificate programs, and more than 200 fields of study. As of Fall 2025, student enrollment for the Lawrence-Edwards campus was 27,212 representing all 50 states and 115 countries. KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe. At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate and support each other’s success. We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued. When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. by the effective date of the appointment. Job Description: 40%  Teaching Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in architectural engineering. Develop courses and participate in curriculum development. Supervise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students throughout the course of their studies. 40%  Research Develop and conduct an innovative research program in building electrical power systems or one of the broader energy areas associated with the positions. Relevant areas include building electrical power systems; integrated building or district energy systems; building-to-grid integration and grid-interactive buildings; distributed energy resource management; grid modernization; building energy resilience; building energy efficiency and decarbonization; environmentally sustainable buildings; renewable energy systems for buildings and communities; grid reliability and energy infrastructure; and net-zero-energy buildings. Establish a strong research agenda leading to external grant support and publication in recognized refereed journals, and participate in local, national, and international professional conferences and other activities appropriate to the discipline. Supervise M.S. and Ph.D. studies. 20%  Service Perform service activities normally expected of university faculty commensurate with rank, including committee service to the Department, School, and University. Also includes national and international service to the profession. Required Qualifications All ranks: Earned doctorate in architectural, mechanical, electrical, or civil engineering or a closely-related discipline by the time of appointment. Demonstrable expertise in one or more of the following areas: building electrical power systems; integrated building or district energy systems; building-to-grid integration and grid-interactive buildings; distributed energy resource management; grid modernization; building energy resilience; building energy efficiency and decarbonization; environmentally sustainable buildings; renewable energy systems for buildings and communities; grid reliability and energy infrastructure; or net-zero-energy buildings. Candidates considered for the building electrical power systems position must demonstrate expertise in that area and the ability to contribute to architectural engineering coursework in building electrical systems and power engineering. Ability to communicate ideas effectively and work well with others as demonstrated through application materials and interview. Demonstrated potential to develop an externally-funded research program and to design and teach high-quality undergraduate and graduate courses. At the rank of Associate Professor: Sustained record of scholarship and research; Evidence of national recognition in the field; Demonstrated experience in leading multidisciplinary teams; A minimum of five years of post-Ph.D. professional experience in a faculty role or an equivalent combination of academic and relevant industry/practice experience. At the rank of Professor: Sustained record of scholarship and research; Evidence of national and international recognition in the field; Demonstrated experience in leading multidisciplinary teams; A minimum of ten years of post-Ph.D. professional experience in a faculty role or an equivalent combination of academic and relevant industry/practice experience, with a sustained record of leadership. Additional Candidate Instructions A complete online application includes the following materials: (1) cover letter, including an indication of the position or positions for which the applicant wishes to be considered (2) curriculum vitae; (3) research agenda (2-page limit); (4) teaching philosophy statement (2-page limit); and (5) the names and contact information, including email addresses, for three professional references. Application review will begin November 13, 2026, and will continue until a pool of qualified applicants is received. Contact Information to Applicants Will Lindquist wlindq10@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range Commensurate with experience Application Review Begins Friday November 13, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Wednesday August 18, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33929BR Department: &nbspCivil/Environ/Arch Engineering Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspOn-Site Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp13-Nov-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
    },
    "https://employment.ku.edu/jobs/faculty/assoc-director-head-of-kenneth-spencer-research-library-public-servcs/33709br": {
      "title": "Assoc. Director/Head of Kenneth Spencer Research Library Public Servcs",
      "description": "Job Posting - Assoc. Director/Head of Kenneth Spencer Research Library Public Servcs Job Posting | Work at KU Skip to main content The University of Kansas MENU link to menu page THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS Work at KU myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS Close Search this unit Start search Submit Search Main navigation Home Jobs Select to follow link Faculty, Lecturers, and Academic Staff Staff Students Application Status How to Apply Frequently Asked Questions Benefits Culture at KU Why KU? Contact Us Info for Prospective Students Current Students Alumni Degree Programs myKU Email Canvas Enroll & Pay Jayhawk GPS THE UNIVERSITY of KANSAS University of Kansas logo Work at KU Menu link to menu page Search this unit Start search Submit Search Home All Jobs Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Jobs Job Posting Short Assoc. Director/Head of Kenneth Spencer Research Library Public Servcs Position Overview This faculty position plays a lead role in bringing the outstanding collections of Kenneth Spencer Research Library (KSRL) to the world, and in bringing KU, Kansas, and the world to our library, virtually and in person. The Associate Director/Kenneth Spencer Research Library Head directs public services, instruction, and educational outreach, coordinating and collaborating with a group of dedicated colleagues. They share best practices in primary source literacy, building a culture of assessment for instruction. They maintain public facing communications, oversee KSRL’s exhibit program, and work in partnership to promote the library on social media and through events and activities. Reporting to the Director, Kenneth Spencer Research Library / Executive Associate Dean for Distinctive Collections, this position supervises three staff members. KU Libraries advance research, teaching, and learning at the intersection of people and ideas. KSRL serves a wide range of users from K-12 students to visiting international researchers, welcoming over 5400 people in person in 2025, including over 700 researchers and 2100 KU student visits. The Stokstad Reading Room is open to the public and does not require appointments, and undertakes significant remote reference and digitization year round. KU’s excellence is a result of the rich tapestry of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds of our faculty, staff, students, and colleagues from across our nation and the globe.  At KU, we invest in continuous learning and growth by creating a climate where people engage in respectful dialogue and debate and support each other’s success.  We foster a culture of care where each person is seen, heard and valued.  When people feel a true sense of belonging, we believe they are better able to reach their full potential and achieve remarkable things. This position is subject to a collective bargaining unit between The University of Kansas and United Academics, that UAKU is the exclusive representative for the bargaining unit, and that it is their right to be a member or not be a member of a union without fear of personal or professional repercussions. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the U.S. by the effective date of the appointment. This is a full-time, 12-month, tenure-track faculty position. Rank considerations are based on professional performance, research, and service experience sufficient to qualify for appointment at the rank of Assistant Librarian or Associate Librarian. See the Libraries Criteria for Academic Ranks document: http://policy.ku.edu/libraries/criteria-academic-ranks-library-faculty Additional information about the Libraries promotion and tenure procedures can be found at the following website: https://policy.ku.edu/ku-libraries/promotion-and-tenure-procedures-faculty Job Description Professional Performance: 45% Develops and implement policies and procedures for reference and access that balance security and preservation with a strong desire to remove barriers Manages a suite of tools to assist students and researchers in using our collections, including the library’s website, tutorials, and guides Serves on appropriate teams, task forces, and groups within the KU Libraries and KU. Participates in a variety of outreach events, special visits, and other relevant occasions, including occasional night and weekend commitments Works collaboratively as a member of project teams and other groups to advance the libraries’ strategic priorities Serves as public contact point for overall KSRL operations, and may work with media and donors to promote our collections, services, and programming Public Services Leadership/Administrative Duties 25% Provides administrative oversight for reference, access, instruction, outreach, exhibits, public-facing communications, and related public services functions at Kenneth Spencer Research Library. Directs three staff members and a sizeable cohort of student assistants, supporting effective coordination, accountability, communication, and professional development. Develops, implements, and maintains policies, procedures, documentation, and service standards for reference, access, reading room services, instruction, outreach, and related public-facing activities. Coordinates instruction program, inviting KU instructors to share Spencer’s collections with their classes, and shares teaching responsibilities with colleagues across the library Establishes goals, priorities, timelines, and operational plans for public services, instruction, outreach, exhibits, and related engagement activities. Uses assessment, user feedback, service data, and other evidence to evaluate public services operations and recommend improvements. Coordinates with the Director, Kenneth Spencer Research Library / Executive Associate Dean for Distinctive Collections, on priorities, planning, reporting, resource needs, and operational concerns. Collaborates with colleagues across Spencer Research Library and KU Libraries to align public services operations with collection stewardship, teaching, research, access, and engagement priorities. Contributes to planning for technology, access, and operational needs related to public services and user engagement. RESEARCH 20% Contributes to the profession’s collective knowledge through scholarly and creative activity. Research may include the publication of articles, books, book reviews, grant-supported inquiry, editorial work, and related forms of dissemination consistent with KU expectations for librarians. SERVICE 10% Engages in service to KU Libraries, the University, and professional organizations through committee work, projects, and other meaningful contributions. This allocation is consistent with KU Libraries’ expectation that librarians maintain a record of service beyond their assigned professional responsibilities. Required Qualifications ALA-accredited master’s degree in Library/Information Science or equivalent from a foreign institution by date of appointment OR a PhD in library and information science from an LIS program that also offered an ALA-accredited master’s degree at the time the PhD was awarded. Demonstrated supervisory experience equivalent to at least two (2) years, including responsibility for leading, mentoring, and evaluating staff performance. In the closely related, specialized field of archives, a graduate degree (may be master’s or Ph.D. depending on position requirements) in an appropriate field OR archival certification by the Academy of Certified Archivists may be considered for a recognized exception. Appointment at the Associate Librarian rank requires a review of the candidate’s materials to ensure work at the level documented in KU Libraries Criteria for Academic Ranks of Library Faculty This position requires a formal degree in the cited discipline area(s) to ensure that candidates have the foundational knowledge and skill set required to perform the duties of the position. The level of degree is based on accreditation requirements, institutional standards of academic and professional excellence, alignment with peer institution benchmarks, and contributes to the credibility and effectiveness of the role within the university community Preferred Qualifications Minimum of two years of experience delivering public-facing reference, research, or access services in an archival, special collections, historical society, or comparable environment. Additional Candidate Instructions Applicants should attach a cover letter, resume/CV, and contact information for three professional references (including at least one previous or current supervisor) to this online application. Please use your cover letter to address how you meet the required and preferred qualifications.  An online application must be completed to be considered for this position. Application deadline is October 14th, 2026 - to ensure consideration please apply before this date. Contact Information to Applicants Mike Broadwell mikebr@ku.edu Advertised Salary Range Minimum $70,000 for Assistant Librarian rank, commensurate with experience. An additional administrative supplement will be provided for supervisory and administrative responsibilities. Application Review Begins Thursday October 15, 2026 Anticipated Start Date Wednesday January 6, 2027 Apply to Job Search Jobs Search all jobs Start search Posting Information Posting ID: &nbsp33709BR Department: &nbspLibraries-General Primary Campus: &nbspUniversity of Kansas Lawrence Campus Assignment: &nbspHybrid Reg/Temp: &nbspRegular Employee Class: &nbspF-Faculty Review Begins: &nbsp15-Oct-2026 Apply to this job Existing Candidate Login Jobs by Type Faculty, Instructional, and Academic Staff Staff Students Other Employment at KU Athletics KU Dining & Memorial Unions Endowment Alumni KUMC Off-Campus Student Employment Related Links Employment Posters Human Resources Immigration Related Notices Civil Rights and Title IX KUMC Human Resources Carruth O'Leary Hall 1246 West Campus Road, Room 101 Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7521 employ@ku.edu 785-864-4946 facebook instagram twitter youtube linkedin Human Resources New Hires at KU Benefits Visit KU KU Admissions KU Endowment KU News KU Events KU Careers KU Alumni Nondiscrimination statement Accessibility | Website support | CMS login © 2026 The University of Kansas The University of Kansas prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, sex, national origin, age, ancestry, disability, status as a veteran, sexual orientation, marital status, parental status, gender identity, gender expression, and genetic information in the university's programs and activities. Retaliation is also prohibited by university policy. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the nondiscrimination policies and procedures and is the Title IX Coordinator for all KU and KUMC campuses: Associate Vice Chancellor for the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX, civilrights@ku.edu , Room 1082, Dole Human Development Center, 1000 Sunnyside Avenue, Lawrence, KS 66045, 785-864-6414 , 711 TTY. Reports can be submitted by contacting the Title IX Coordinator as provided herein or using the Title IX online report form and complaints can be submitted with the Title IX Coordinator or using the Title IX online complaint form . The University of Kansas is a public institution governed by the Kansas Board of Regents."
